###### Abstract

Fast radio bursts (FRBs) have excessive dispersion measures (DMs) and an all-sky distribution, which point toward an extragalactic or even a cosmological origin. We develop a method to extract the mean host galaxy DM (\\langleDM{}_{\\rm HG,loc}\\rangle) and the characterized luminosity (L) of FRBs using the observed DM-Flux data, based on the assumption of a narrow luminosity distribution. Applying Bayesian inference to the data of 21 FRBs, we derive a relatively large mean host DM, i.e. (DM{}_{\\rm HG,loc}) \\sim 270 pc cm{}^{-3} with a large dispersion. A relatively large DM{}_{\\rm HG} of FRBs is also supported by the millisecond scattering times of some FRBs and the relatively small redshift z=0.19273 of FRB 121102 (which gives DM{}_{\\rm HG,loc}\\sim 210 pc cm{}^{-3}). The large host galaxy DM may be contributed by the ISM or a near-source plasma in the host galaxy. If it is contributed by the ISM, the type of the FRB host galaxies would not be Milky Way (MW)-like, consistent with the detected host of FRB 121102. We also discuss the possibility of having a near-source supernova remnant (SNR), pulsar wind nebula (PWN) or HII region that gives a significant contribution to the observed DM{}_{\\rm HG}.

Subject headings:intergalactic medium -- radio continuum: general

## 1. Introduction

Fast radio bursts (FRBs) are mysterious astronomical radio transients with short intrinsic durations (\\sim 1ms), large dispersion measures (DM \\gtrsim 200 pc cm{}^{-3}), and an all-sky distribution (Lorimer et al., 2007; Keane et al., 2012, 2016; Thornton et al., 2013; Burke-Spolaor & Bannister, 2014; Spitler et al., 2014, 2016; Masui et al., 2015; Petroff et al., 2015; Ravi et al., 2015, 2016; Champion et al., 2016; Chatterjee et al., 2017; Caleb et al., 2017). Recently, thanks to the precise localization and multi-wavelength follow-up observations of the repeating source FRB 121102 (Chatterjee et al., 2017; Marcote et al., 2017; Tendulkar et al., 2017), the distance scale of FRBs has been finally settled to a cosmological scale at z=0.19273(Tendulkar et al., 2017). The large DM excess of other FRBs with respect to the Galactic value and their high Galactic latitudes also suggest that most, if not all, FRBs should have an extragalactic (e.g. \\sim 10 Mpc), and likely a cosmological (e.g. >100 Mpc) origin.

The host galaxies of FRBs carry important information regarding the progenitor of FRBs. For FRB 121102, optical imaging and spectroscopy indicate a dwarf galaxy with a mass of M\\sim(4-7)\\times 10^{7}M_{\\odot} as the host galaxy. The H{}_{\\alpha} flux of the host galaxy suggests a star formation rate of SFR \\sim 0.4M_{\\odot}yr{}^{-1}(Tendulkar et al., 2017). No information about the host galaxies of other FRBs is available. One possible way to derive FRB host galaxy information is to extract the host galaxy DM from data. Yang & Zhang (2016) proposed a method to derive DM{}_{\\rm HG} using the measured DM and z of a sample of FRBs. However, the z values of most FRBs are not obtained so far.

In this paper, we further develop a method to apply DM and flux of FRBs to infer DM{}_{\\rm HG}. This method is applied to the current FRB sample with 21 sources. Through Bayesian inference, we derive a relatively large mean host galaxy DM, \\langleDM{}_{\\rm HG,loc}), for FRBs. We also provide two pieces of supporting evidence for a large value of DM{}_{\\rm HG}: millisecond-duration of scattering tails for some FRBs and DM{}_{\\rm HG,loc}\\sim 210 pc cm{}^{-3} for FRB 121102.

## 2. Method

For an FRB, the observed dispersion measure has three contributions (e.g. Lorimer et al., 2007; Keane et al., 2012; Thornton et al., 2013; Deng & Zhang, 2014; Gao et al., 2014; Yang & Zhang, 2016; Murase et al., 2016a), i.e.

{\\rm DM}_{\\rm obs}={\\rm DM}_{\\rm MW}+{\\rm DM}_{\\rm HG}+{\\rm DM}_{\\rm IGM},  which are from the Milky Way, the FRB host galaxy (which itself includes the contributions from the interstellar medium (ISM) in the host galaxy and a near-source plasma), and the IGM, respectively. According to the Galactic pulsar data, DM{}_{\\rm MW} can be estimated for a localized FRB (Cordes & Lazio, 2003), so one can define the extragalactic (or excess) dispersion measure of an FRB as

{\\rm DM}_{\\rm E}\\equiv{\\rm DM}_{\\rm obs}-{\\rm DM}_{\\rm MW}={\\rm DM}_{\\rm IGM} +{\\rm DM}_{\\rm HG},  which can be treated as an observed quantity. The local DMs of FRB host galaxies may be assumed to have no significant evolution with redshift of z\\lesssim 1, i.e. \\langleDM{}_{\\rm HG,loc}\\rangle\\sim const, where \\langleDM{}_{\\rm HG,loc}\\rangle is the average value of the rest-frame host galaxy DM within a certain redshift bin. Due to cosmological time dilation, the observed host DM value reads DM{}_{\\rm HG}={\\rm DM}_{\\rm HG,loc}/(1+z)(Ioka, 2003). Considering the local inhomogeneity of the IGM (McQuinn, 2014), we define a mean DM of the IGMas (Deng & Zhang, 2014; Yang & Zhang, 2016)

\\langle{\\rm DM}_{\\rm IGM}\\rangle = \\frac{3cH_{0}\\Omega_{b}f_{\\rm IGM}}{8\\pi Gm_{p}}\\int_{0}^{z}\\frac{ f_{e}(z^{\\prime})(1+z^{\\prime})}{\\sqrt{\\Omega_{m}(1+z^{\\prime})^{3}+\\Omega_{\\Lambda}}}dz^{ \\prime}, \\simeq \\frac{3cH_{0}\\Omega_{b}f_{\\rm IGM}f_{e}}{8\\pi Gm_{p}}\\left[z+z^{2} \\left(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3\\Omega_{m}}{4}\\right)+O(z^{2})\\right], where f_{e}(z)=(3/4)y_{1}\\chi_{e,{\\rm H}}(z)+(1/8)y_{2}\\chi_{e,{\\rm He}}(z), y_{1}\\sim 1 and y_{2}\\simeq 4-3y_{1}\\sim 1 are the hydrogen and helium mass fractions normalized to 3/4 and 1/4, respectively, and \\chi_{e,{\\rm H}}(z) and \\chi_{e,{\\rm He}}(z) are the ionization fractions for hydrogen and helium, respectively. For z<3, one has \\chi_{e,{\\rm H}}(z)\\simeq\\chi_{e,{\\rm He}}(z)\\simeq 1, due to full ionization of both hydrogen and helium (Meiksin, 2009). Therefore, one has f_{e}(z)\\simeq f_{e}=7/8. We adopt the flat \\LambdaCDM parameters recently derived from the _Planck_ data: H_{0}=67.7 km s{}^{-1}Mpc{}^{-1},~{}\\Omega_{m}=0.31,~{}\\Omega_{\\Lambda}=0.69, and \\Omega_{b}=0.049(Planck Collaboration et al., 2015). For the fraction of baryon mass in the intergalactic medium, we adopt f_{\\rm IGM}=0.83(Fukugita et al., 1998; Shull et al., 2012). In fact, due to IGM inhomogeneity, the fluctuation of individual measurements is expected (McQuinn, 2014). Since the formation and evolution of the different galaxies are essentially independent at a given redshift z, {\\rm DM}_{\\rm IGM}(z) for different lines of sight would have a Gaussian distribution. As a result, the inhomogeneity of the IGM may affect the scatter, but not the mean trend of the DM-flux relation, especially when the sample size is large enough.

For an FRB with an intrinsic frequency-dependent isotropic-equivalent luminosity L_{\\nu_{*}}(\\nu_{*}), the observed flux is given by F_{\\nu}d\\nu=L_{\\nu_{*}}d\\nu_{*}/4\\pi d_{L}^{2}. The luminosity distance of the FRB may be given by

d_{L}\\simeq\\left(\\frac{L_{\\rm iso}}{4\\pi\\nu F_{\\nu}}\\right)^{1/2},  where L_{\\rm iso}\\equiv\\nu_{*}L_{\\nu_{*}} is the characteristic isotropic-equivalent luminosity (The true luminosity should be L=(\\Delta\\Omega/4\\pi)L_{\\rm iso}, where \\Delta\\Omega is the beaming solid angle), and \\nu\\simeq 1.4 GHz is the characteristic frequency of FRBs1. For a flat universe, one has

Footnote 1: Strictly speaking, a proper k-correction is needed to derive a more rigorous d_{L}. However, the FRB spectral shape is not well constrained. Since the FRB emission seems to peak around 1 GHz and since the FRB redshift is not very high, our approximate treatment is justified.

d_{L} = \\frac{c}{H_{0}}(1+z)\\int_{0}^{z}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\Omega_{m}(1+z^{ \\prime})^{3}+\\Omega_{\\Lambda}}}dz^{\\prime},  \\simeq \\frac{c}{H_{0}}\\left[z+z^{2}\\left(1-\\frac{3\\Omega_{m}}{4}\\right) +O(z^{2})\\right].

For z\\lesssim 1, according to Eq.(2)-Eq.(5), we can obtain approximately {\\rm DM}_{\\rm IGM}\\simeq Ad_{L}, where A\\equiv 3H_{0}^{2}\\Omega_{b}f_{\\rm IGM}f_{e}/8\\pi Gm_{p}. Therefore, one has the {\\rm DM}_{\\rm E}-F_{\\nu} relation:

\\langle{\\rm DM}_{\\rm E}\\rangle\\simeq\\frac{A}{\\sqrt{4\\pi}}L_{\\rm iso}^{1/2}\\nu^ {-1/2}F_{\\nu}^{-1/2}+\\langle{\\rm DM}_{\\rm HG,loc}\\rangle\\,. 

As shown in Eq.(6), \\langle{\\rm DM}_{\\rm E}\\rangle\\propto F_{\\nu}^{-1/2} for F_{\\nu}\\ll F_{\\nu,{\\rm crit}}, and \\langle{\\rm DM}_{\\rm E}\\rangle\\simeq\\langle{\\rm DM}_{\\rm HG,loc}\\rangle for F_{\\nu}\\gg F_{\\nu,{\\rm crit}}, where F_{\\nu,{\\rm crit}}\\equiv A^{2}L_{\\rm iso}/4\\pi\\nu\\,\\langle{\\rm DM}_{\\rm HG, loc}\\rangle^{2}.

One can numerically solve Eq.(2)-Eq.(5), and use the observed {\\rm DM}_{\\rm E}-F_{\\nu} relation to fit the current sample of 21 FRBs2. We take the FRB data from the _FRB Catalogue_ of Petroff et al. (2016)3 and ignore the effect of interstellar scintillation. Except FRB 121102, other FRBs (mostly detected with Parkes) are not observed to repeat. If all FRB sources repeat, most bursts may be below the sensitivity of the Parkes telescope, and the detected one may be one of the brightest pulses. For this reason, we take the brightest pulse of the repeater to define its peak flux at 1.4 GHz.4

Footnote 2: The three bursts reported by Caleb et al. (2017), similar to the original “lorimer” burst (Lorimer et al., 2007), only have the lower limits of the peak fluxes reported. In our analysis, these lower limits are used. Our conclusion of a large host galaxy DM remains valid if one adopts larger peak flux values for these bursts.

